WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:17.520 --> 00:00:19.610
Jeremy Bentham was a man ahead of his time.

00:00:19.610 --> 00:00:25.540
Though not a philosopher, he developed a utilitarian
philosophy based on the doing of good to oneself

00:00:25.540 --> 00:00:26.540
and others.

00:00:26.540 --> 00:00:31.520
He used it to propose fundamental reforms
to a system that he saw as grossly unjust.

00:00:31.520 --> 00:00:36.680
His ideas have reverberated down through the
ages to affect the world that we live in today.

00:00:36.680 --> 00:00:43.030
In today’s Biographix, we delve into the
life and teachings of Jeremy Bentham.

00:00:43.030 --> 00:00:50.940
A Child Prodigy
Jeremy Bentham was born On February 15th,

00:00:50.940 --> 00:00:52.820
1748 in Houndsditch, London.

00:00:52.820 --> 00:00:57.340
The Bentham’s were a family of wealth, with
income derived from an extremely successful

00:00:57.340 --> 00:01:00.500
pawnbroking enterprise in generations past.

00:01:00.500 --> 00:01:04.620
His parents, James and Alice, spent their
time in social and intellectual pursuits.

00:01:04.620 --> 00:01:09.100
Young Bentham was a child prodigy, with his
extraordinary abilities becoming evident very

00:01:09.100 --> 00:01:10.100
early on.

00:01:10.100 --> 00:01:12.340
By the age of three he was reading fluently.

00:01:12.340 --> 00:01:16.990
On one occasion, when he had just turned three,
he was walking in the woods with his parents

00:01:16.990 --> 00:01:17.990
and some other adults.

00:01:17.990 --> 00:01:21.580
He became bored with the adult conversation
and decided to return home.

00:01:21.580 --> 00:01:25.410
As the adults were absorbed in their conversation,
they didn’t notice his departure.

00:01:25.410 --> 00:01:29.209
When they returned home, the adults found
him sitting at a reading desk, absorbed in

00:01:29.209 --> 00:01:31.860
reading a book on the history of England.

00:01:31.860 --> 00:01:34.520
Jeremy’s classical studies began at the
age of four.

00:01:34.520 --> 00:01:38.300
Among the journals of his father, there exists
a piece of paper with the following on it

00:01:38.300 --> 00:01:41.550
. . .
The line pasted herein was written by my son,

00:01:41.550 --> 00:01:46.380
Jeremy Bentham, the 4th of December 1753,
at the age of five years, nine months and

00:01:46.380 --> 00:01:47.530
nineteen days.

00:01:47.530 --> 00:01:50.930
The line in question was a long phrase in
Latin.

00:01:50.930 --> 00:01:54.550
This document shows, not only the amazing
talent of the child, but also the obvious

00:01:54.550 --> 00:01:58.160
pride that the father had in his son’s intellectual
gifts.

00:01:58.160 --> 00:02:02.650
In fact, James, was constantly bragging about
the genius that lay within his boy.

00:02:02.650 --> 00:02:06.470
At age four, Jeremy was learning both the
Greek and Latin languages.

00:02:06.470 --> 00:02:11.110
James employed a clerk and this man was given
the duty of teaching young James to write

00:02:11.110 --> 00:02:13.590
as well as to read music and play the violin.

00:02:13.590 --> 00:02:17.700
By the age of six, a regular music tutor had
been employed and James, with his miniature

00:02:17.700 --> 00:02:20.140
fiddle, became an adept violinist.

00:02:20.140 --> 00:02:23.170
James wanted for his son to excel at everything.

00:02:23.170 --> 00:02:25.700
When Jeremy was seven, he was given dancing
lessons.

00:02:25.700 --> 00:02:28.060
It was something that he absolutely detested.

00:02:28.060 --> 00:02:31.540
The boy was so weak that he was unable to
support himself on his tiptoes.

00:02:31.540 --> 00:02:36.790
Yet, his father repeatedly forced him to go
through the exercises, becoming more exasperated

00:02:36.790 --> 00:02:38.510
with every failed attempt.

00:02:38.510 --> 00:02:41.950
When not absorbed in his studies with his
father, Jeremy loved nothing better than to

00:02:41.950 --> 00:02:45.800
spend time at the country estates of his two
grandmothers at Browning Hill and Barking.

00:02:45.800 --> 00:02:53.660
He was a lover and nature and would spend
hours exploring the flowers and trees of the

00:02:53.660 --> 00:02:56.800
family grounds.

00:02:56.800 --> 00:03:00.020
Westminster
While young James was mentally superior to

00:03:00.020 --> 00:03:02.930
his peers, physically he was anything about.

00:03:02.930 --> 00:03:07.070
He was much shorter than other children of
his age and was extremely thin and weak.

00:03:07.070 --> 00:03:10.730
When he was seven years of age, he began attending
Westminster School in London.

00:03:10.730 --> 00:03:14.930
He immediately hated the experience, it being
a far contrast to the one on one instruction

00:03:14.930 --> 00:03:16.720
he had been receiving almost since birth.

00:03:16.720 --> 00:03:20.380
James later wrote . . .
Westminster was a wretched place for instruction.

00:03:20.380 --> 00:03:24.510
I was the least boy in the school, and the
bigger boys took pleasure in pitting us one

00:03:24.510 --> 00:03:25.720
against another.

00:03:25.720 --> 00:03:29.740
Bentham was entered into the upper second
form due to his obvious advanced intellect.

00:03:29.740 --> 00:03:32.870
He soon gained a reputation as ‘the little
philosopher’ as a result of his ability

00:03:32.870 --> 00:03:35.180
to reason and consider every matter.

00:03:35.180 --> 00:03:39.430
His skills in Latin and Greek made him a valuable
boy to know and many of the other boys leaned

00:03:39.430 --> 00:03:41.270
on him to provide homework answers.

00:03:41.270 --> 00:03:43.810
Bentham soon became a target for bullies at
the school.

00:03:43.810 --> 00:03:46.460
However, there was one boy who served as a
protector for him.

00:03:46.460 --> 00:03:50.790
If it wasn’t for this companionship, he
would have been the subject of daily humiliations.

00:03:50.790 --> 00:03:55.250
In his older years he spoke with disgust at
the fagging system by which the younger boys

00:03:55.250 --> 00:03:57.740
were subjected to all manner of intolerant
treatment.

00:03:57.740 --> 00:04:02.000
He was just as dismissive of the teaching
that was offered at the school, saying that

00:04:02.000 --> 00:04:05.900
he was taught few useful and many useless
things.

00:04:05.900 --> 00:04:11.570
Bentham observed the higher the rank of a
professor, the less efficient that man was.

00:04:11.570 --> 00:04:18.850
On to Oxford
Bentham persevered with the agony and boredom

00:04:18.850 --> 00:04:20.640
of Westminster for five years.

00:04:20.640 --> 00:04:24.850
Finally, at age twelve he graduated and his
father enrolled him at Queens College in Oxford.

00:04:24.850 --> 00:04:28.690
It was a remarkable achievement for a such
a young man to be enrolled at university.

00:04:28.690 --> 00:04:32.490
The fact that Bentham was extremely short
for a twelve-year-old made him an even greater

00:04:32.490 --> 00:04:35.860
curiosity than usual and he was stared at
wherever he went.

00:04:35.860 --> 00:04:39.389
In addition to developing an extraordinary
intellectual body of knowledge, by the time

00:04:39.389 --> 00:04:43.960
he was a teenager, Bentham had also built
for himself a strong moral code.

00:04:43.960 --> 00:04:45.810
He recalled . . .
I never told a lie.

00:04:45.810 --> 00:04:49.610
I never, in my remembrance, did what I knew
to be a dishonest thing.

00:04:49.610 --> 00:04:53.680
His father placed him under the care of a
personal tutor by the name of Jacob Jefferson.

00:04:53.680 --> 00:04:58.669
He was a morose and gloomy person who saw
it as his duty to prevent Jeremy from having

00:04:58.669 --> 00:05:01.889
anything that resembled fun.

00:05:01.889 --> 00:05:05.629
The only thing that remotely approached fun
in Jeremy’s life was to play shuttlecock

00:05:05.629 --> 00:05:06.800
very irregularly.

00:05:06.800 --> 00:05:10.800
Yet, even this was unacceptable to Jefferson
and he would interrupt the game and usher

00:05:10.800 --> 00:05:12.159
the boy back to his books.

00:05:12.159 --> 00:05:17.360
He instilled in Bentham’s psyche that any
activity that did involve educational advancement

00:05:17.360 --> 00:05:18.490
was a waste of time.

00:05:18.490 --> 00:05:22.380
While at Oxford, Bentham received only a modest
allowance from his father.

00:05:22.380 --> 00:05:24.881
This meant that he was forced to incur debt,
to his great horror.

00:05:24.881 --> 00:05:29.139
His father was also concerned that the boy
was too timid and shy.

00:05:29.139 --> 00:05:32.669
On one occasion, while visiting Oxford, James
took his son into the grand hall at Christ

00:05:32.669 --> 00:05:33.669
Church.

00:05:33.669 --> 00:05:36.210
At the time, the students there were all assembled
for dinner.

00:05:36.210 --> 00:05:40.479
James Instructed his son to walk up and down
the aisles and see if he could find any boys

00:05:40.479 --> 00:05:41.479
that he knew.

00:05:41.479 --> 00:05:44.599
For Jeremy this was a hellish assignment.

00:05:44.599 --> 00:05:47.489
With each succeeding step he felt closer to
fainting.

00:05:47.489 --> 00:05:52.009
By the time he had finished, he was red with
embarrassment and had no acquaintances to

00:05:52.009 --> 00:05:53.009
report.

00:05:53.009 --> 00:05:56.300
His father, though, thought this was an excellent
strategy for his son to get noticed.

00:05:56.300 --> 00:06:00.669
Further to that goal, he bought a silk gown
for Jeremy, while the other boys wore plain

00:06:00.669 --> 00:06:01.669
gowns.

00:06:01.669 --> 00:06:06.430
Bentham found the instruction at Oxford almost
as pointless and uninspiring as he had at

00:06:06.430 --> 00:06:07.430
Westminster.

00:06:07.430 --> 00:06:11.120
He managed, at times, to escape the monotony
of his routine with fishing, which he was

00:06:11.120 --> 00:06:12.509
not particularly fond of.

00:06:12.509 --> 00:06:16.469
He found no pleasure in the company of any
other student, considering the vast majority

00:06:16.469 --> 00:06:18.449
of them to be immature morons.

00:06:18.449 --> 00:06:21.389
When he was sixteen, Jeremy’s father took
him on a tour of France.

00:06:21.389 --> 00:06:25.639
He was fascinated by the beauty and architecture
of Paris, being especially impressed by the

00:06:25.639 --> 00:06:27.150
wonders of the Palace of Versailles.

00:06:27.150 --> 00:06:29.040
Shortly thereafter, his father remarried.

00:06:29.040 --> 00:06:33.689
Jeremy’s step mother, the former Mrs Abbott,
had a strained relationship with Bentham and

00:06:33.689 --> 00:06:35.740
he also spoke of her with a lack of affection.

00:06:35.740 --> 00:06:39.669
In 1766, Bentham took his master’s degree
at Oxford, with a major in law.

00:06:39.669 --> 00:06:43.190
To celebrate his graduation his father gave
him a gift of twenty pounds.

00:06:43.190 --> 00:06:45.039
The following year he left Oxford.

00:06:45.039 --> 00:06:48.139
In his own estimation his education at the
college had not benefited him.

00:06:48.139 --> 00:06:49.139
One of his father’s ambitions for him had
failed to materialize.

00:06:49.139 --> 00:06:52.050
James had intended that Jeremy would use the
opportunities at the college to establish

00:06:52.050 --> 00:06:56.169
contacts that he would be able to exploit
upon graduation in order to make his way on

00:06:56.169 --> 00:06:57.169
the world.

00:06:57.169 --> 00:07:01.249
But the boy had been unable to shake his natural
timidity and didn’t build any meaningful

00:07:01.249 --> 00:07:08.509
relationships with people who could help him
in the future.

00:07:08.509 --> 00:07:13.759
A New Path
Bentham was admitted to the bar in 1769, though

00:07:13.759 --> 00:07:15.509
he never ended up practicing law.

00:07:15.509 --> 00:07:19.439
He became greatly disappointed with the English
legal code, which he considered to be overly

00:07:19.439 --> 00:07:21.740
cumbersome and complex.

00:07:21.740 --> 00:07:26.710
His disillusionment was compounded upon hearing
a series of lectures by the leading legal

00:07:26.710 --> 00:07:29.270
authority of the time, Sir William Blackstone.

00:07:29.270 --> 00:07:32.999
After listening to Blackstone, he decided
that he would no longer practice law.

00:07:32.999 --> 00:07:34.710
Instead he would write about it.

00:07:34.710 --> 00:07:38.789
He would spend his energies on critiquing
the existing law and proposing ways that it

00:07:38.789 --> 00:07:39.979
could be improved upon.

00:07:39.979 --> 00:07:43.629
Bentham took another tour of France with his
father and step-mother in the latter half

00:07:43.629 --> 00:07:44.629
of 1769.

00:07:44.629 --> 00:07:48.699
In 1770, Bentham started upon his extensive
writing career.

00:07:48.699 --> 00:07:53.159
His first published works were two letters
sent to the London Gazetteer when he was twenty-three.

00:07:53.159 --> 00:07:57.869
Of one of these letters he later said . . .
It was a portrait of my character and my love

00:07:57.869 --> 00:08:01.589
of fairness . . . Lord Mansfield had been
attacked . . . I showed that there was no

00:08:01.589 --> 00:08:03.110
evidence for the story.

00:08:03.110 --> 00:08:06.650
Bentham spent an inordinate amount of time
perfecting every word that he committed to

00:08:06.650 --> 00:08:07.650
paper.

00:08:07.650 --> 00:08:08.650
Everything had to be precise.

00:08:08.650 --> 00:08:13.069
His early writings often contain the seeds
of the ideas that were fully fleshed out in

00:08:13.069 --> 00:08:15.240
his more famous later works.

00:08:15.240 --> 00:08:18.669
Taking on the Americans

00:08:18.669 --> 00:08:25.069
In July, 1776, the American colonies issued
their declaration of Independence.

00:08:25.069 --> 00:08:28.349
The British government did not lower themselves
to issue an official response.

00:08:28.349 --> 00:08:32.370
However, a London based pamphleteer was commissioned
to publish a rebuttal.

00:08:32.370 --> 00:08:36.349
The man who was charged with writing the piece
was Jeremy Bentham.

00:08:36.349 --> 00:08:41.270
However, the response was not published under
Bentham’s name but was the final chapter

00:08:41.270 --> 00:08:44.390
of John Lind’s Answer to the Declaration
of Independence.

00:08:44.390 --> 00:08:48.440
Bentham was a good friend of Lind and was
living at his house when Lind was commissioned

00:08:48.440 --> 00:08:49.440
to the job.

00:08:49.440 --> 00:08:51.560
He was delighted to take on the Americans
in print.

00:08:51.560 --> 00:08:55.720
He proceeded to pull apart the Declaration,
ruthlessly exposing its weak points.

00:08:55.720 --> 00:08:58.190
He sets the tone in his introduction, declaring
. . .

00:08:58.190 --> 00:09:03.100
The opinions of the modern Americans on Government,
like those of their good ancestors on witchcraft,

00:09:03.100 --> 00:09:08.790
would be too ridiculous to deserve any notice,
if like them too, contemptible and extravagant

00:09:08.790 --> 00:09:12.540
as they be, they had not led to the most serious
evils.

00:09:12.540 --> 00:09:16.790
Among his many critiques, Bentham ridicules
the idea of all men being created equal, stating

00:09:16.790 --> 00:09:20.621
that it gives equal status to the newborn
baby and to the magistrate.

00:09:20.621 --> 00:09:24.570
He also claims that the use of Government
to ensure the attainment of life, liberty

00:09:24.570 --> 00:09:28.720
and the pursuit of happiness is an oxymoron
– as the exercise of any form of government

00:09:28.720 --> 00:09:32.780
will alienate at least one of those inalienable
rights.

00:09:32.780 --> 00:09:36.930
Bentham dismisses the framers of the Declaration
of Independence as nothing more than a mob

00:09:36.930 --> 00:09:40.260
of radicals, worse even than the German Anabaptists.

00:09:40.260 --> 00:09:45.010
He concludes with sincere wishes that these
‘ungrateful and rebellious people’ be

00:09:45.010 --> 00:09:46.880
forced back into line.

00:09:46.880 --> 00:09:47.880
The Panopticon

00:09:47.880 --> 00:09:49.410
Having dismissed the Americans, Bentham set
his focus on writing about social and legal

00:09:49.410 --> 00:09:50.410
reforms in his beloved England.

00:09:50.410 --> 00:09:52.240
One of his pet projects was a design for an
improved prison building called the Panopticon.

00:09:52.240 --> 00:09:56.370
The design of the structure allowed every
prisoner in the facility to be able to be

00:09:56.370 --> 00:09:59.490
watched by a single guard without them knowing
whether or not they were under observation.

00:09:59.490 --> 00:10:03.090
Bentham was well aware that there was no way
that a single watchman could actually keep

00:10:03.090 --> 00:10:07.290
an eye on everyone at the same time, but the
fact that prisoners didn’t know if he was

00:10:07.290 --> 00:10:09.450
being watched would, according to Bentham,
force him to regulate his own behaviour.

00:10:09.450 --> 00:12:16.460
The Panopticon was a circular building with
a watch-house in the

00:12:16.460 --> 00:12:19.190
center where a guard could monitor all of
the inmates.

00:12:19.190 --> 00:12:23.430
Bentham described the design as ‘a mill
for grinding rogues honest.’

00:12:23.430 --> 00:12:27.160
In 1791, Bentham published a book on his Panopticon
plans.

00:12:27.160 --> 00:12:31.460
He fully intended for the prison to be built
and even planned to govern the facility himself.

00:12:31.460 --> 00:12:35.850
In 1794 he was able to interest the government
of William Pitt in the idea.

00:12:35.850 --> 00:12:38.440
He was paid £2000 for his plans.

00:12:38.440 --> 00:12:42.690
After a number of frustrating hold ups, Bentham
ended up buying a plot of land on behalf of

00:12:42.690 --> 00:12:44.960
the Crown in 1799.

00:12:44.960 --> 00:12:47.660
From his perspective, the site was far from
ideal.

00:12:47.660 --> 00:12:52.490
When he requested funds go buy more land,
he was told to simply build a smaller prison.

00:12:52.490 --> 00:12:55.460
From this he discerned that there was no real
commitment to penal reform on the part of

00:12:55.460 --> 00:12:56.460
the government.

00:12:56.460 --> 00:13:00.380
When William Pitt resigned from Parliament
in 1801, enthusiasm for the project waned.

00:13:00.380 --> 00:13:03.210
Two years later it was shut down completely.

00:13:03.210 --> 00:13:06.790
Bentham was crushed by the closing down of
his pet project, stating . . .

00:13:06.790 --> 00:13:10.550
They have murdered my best days!

00:13:10.550 --> 00:13:12.510
Utilitarianism

00:13:12.510 --> 00:13:19.410
Bentham’s political philosophy is summed
up in the concept of utilitarianism.

00:13:19.410 --> 00:13:24.040
It is based on the principle that an action
is right only if it promotes happiness and

00:13:24.040 --> 00:13:26.620
wrong if it does not promote happiness.

00:13:26.620 --> 00:13:31.390
That happiness relates not only to the instigator
of an action but to the rest of society as

00:13:31.390 --> 00:13:32.390
well.

00:13:32.390 --> 00:13:36.280
Bentham’s answer to the question, what should
a person do? is that a person should do only

00:13:36.280 --> 00:13:39.150
that which produces the best consequences.

00:13:39.150 --> 00:13:41.430
Bentham was not a professional philosopher.

00:13:41.430 --> 00:13:45.550
As a critic and shaper of the law, he believed
that his utilitarian principle should be the

00:13:45.550 --> 00:13:50.880
practical guide by which all people, including
the lawmakers in government, made decisions.

00:13:50.880 --> 00:13:55.250
His underlying principle was summed up by
his contemporary Joseph Priestly as . . .

00:13:55.250 --> 00:13:57.860
The greatest happiness for the greatest number.

00:13:57.860 --> 00:14:01.670
His greatest wish was that the lawmakers of
society would use his principle in order to

00:14:01.670 --> 00:14:05.900
remake a more equitable and logical legal
framework for society.

00:14:05.900 --> 00:14:06.900
He wrote . . .

00:14:06.900 --> 00:14:10.900
If anything could be identified as the fundamental
motivation behind the development of Classical

00:14:10.900 --> 00:14:17.150
Utilitarianism it would be the desire to see
useless, corrupt laws and social practices

00:14:17.150 --> 00:14:18.150
changed.

00:14:18.150 --> 00:14:21.680
In determining what was good and what was
bad, Bentham, believed that the pursuit of

00:14:21.680 --> 00:14:24.890
pleasure and the avoidance of pain were the
deciding factors.

00:14:24.890 --> 00:14:28.730
He held it as a given that pleasure was good
and pain was bad.

00:14:28.730 --> 00:14:32.230
The fact that a person is rich or poor, black
or white, male or female, intelligent or dim

00:14:32.230 --> 00:14:37.040
witted should, he believed have absolutely
no bearing on their right to enjoy the same

00:14:37.040 --> 00:14:41.370
level of pleasure and avoid the same measure
of pain as anyone else.

00:14:41.370 --> 00:14:46.000
In the class defined society that was England
at that time, this was a radical notion.

00:14:46.000 --> 00:14:51.170
Bentham was never interested in anything unless
it could be practically applied in society.

00:14:51.170 --> 00:14:55.461
In order to help people to apply his utilitarian
theory he developed what he called the Hedonic

00:14:55.461 --> 00:14:56.500
Calculus.

00:14:56.500 --> 00:15:00.470
By using it, propose would be able to balance
out the pleasurable and painful effects of

00:15:00.470 --> 00:15:01.580
any proposed action.

00:15:01.580 --> 00:15:06.100
The hedonic calculus includes seven factors
which need to be taken into account in relation

00:15:06.100 --> 00:15:09.340
to the pleasure or pain produced when considering
a course of action.

00:15:09.340 --> 00:15:14.840
These are the intensity, duration, certainty,
remoteness, extent, purity and fecundity or

00:15:14.840 --> 00:15:17.700
likelihood that the sensation will be followed
by more of the same.

00:15:17.700 --> 00:15:22.710
An example of fecundity that Bentham gave
was smiling at a stranger.

00:15:22.710 --> 00:15:25.040
This may make you feel good for a short period
of time.

00:15:25.040 --> 00:15:29.190
But it may also cause that stranger to smile
at someone else and this could keep going

00:15:29.190 --> 00:15:33.350
on indefinitely, so the sensation would be
repeated many times over.

00:15:33.350 --> 00:15:35.540
Purity is the opposite of fecundity.

00:15:35.540 --> 00:15:39.380
We might get a temporary feeling of pleasure
by punching someone in the face, but the chances

00:15:39.380 --> 00:15:44.380
are high that they will punch us right back,
in which case we will get the opposite sensation.

00:15:44.380 --> 00:15:51.460
So, the sensation is not pure, it is quickly
diluted, so to speak.

00:15:51.460 --> 00:15:55.470
The Pool of London

00:15:55.470 --> 00:15:59.370
In the 1790’s, Bentham became interested
in helping to clean up a notorious area known

00:15:59.370 --> 00:16:00.370
as the Pool of London.

00:16:00.370 --> 00:16:04.710
This was a stretch that rang alongside the
River Thames from London Bridge down to Limehouse.

00:16:04.710 --> 00:16:07.779
It was a center of crime, vice and all sorts
of illegal activity.

00:16:07.779 --> 00:16:12.910
A major problem was shipboard theft with merchants
losing half a million pounds of merchandise

00:16:12.910 --> 00:16:13.910
each year.

00:16:13.910 --> 00:16:17.680
Bentham worked with a Scottish magistrate
by the name of Patrick Colquhoun for a solution

00:16:17.680 --> 00:16:18.680
to the problem.

00:16:18.680 --> 00:16:21.970
What they came out out with was the Thames
River Bill, which was passed into law in the

00:16:21.970 --> 00:16:22.970
year 1800.

00:16:22.970 --> 00:16:25.900
It brought into existence the Thames River
Police.

00:16:25.900 --> 00:16:29.980
This force, the first police force in England,
was specifically designed to stop theft and

00:16:29.980 --> 00:16:31.140
looting from ships.

00:16:31.140 --> 00:16:38.430
It was designed to act as a preventive for
crime, which was a new concept at the time.

00:16:38.430 --> 00:16:42.910
International Influence

00:16:42.910 --> 00:16:47.230
By the 1780’s, Bentham’s writings and
influence had made him an influential world

00:16:47.230 --> 00:16:48.230
thinker.

00:16:48.230 --> 00:16:49.630
He was in contact with many people of note.

00:16:49.630 --> 00:16:53.980
He kept up a lively communication with Scottish
economist Adam Smith in an attempt to bring

00:16:53.980 --> 00:16:56.030
about free-floating interest rates.

00:16:56.030 --> 00:16:59.820
He was also in communication with the movers
and shakers of the French revolution.

00:16:59.820 --> 00:17:04.220
However, when things devolved into the mass
terror of wholesale beheadings, he roundly

00:17:04.220 --> 00:17:07.000
criticized what was happening there.

00:17:07.000 --> 00:17:11.909
Animal Rights Advocate

00:17:11.909 --> 00:17:15.740
Bentham is remembered as one of the earliest
advocates of animal rights.

00:17:15.740 --> 00:17:20.360
Extending his utilitarian philosophy, he stated
that it was not the ability to reason, but

00:17:20.360 --> 00:17:26.059
rather the ability to feel which determined
who or what was covered by the pleasure and

00:17:26.059 --> 00:17:27.059
pain principle.

00:17:27.059 --> 00:17:30.850
Bentham did believe that animals could be
killed for food or if a person was defending

00:17:30.850 --> 00:17:35.900
themselves or others from attack, but only
so long as there was no gratuitous suffering

00:17:35.900 --> 00:17:36.900
involved.

00:17:36.900 --> 00:17:40.540
Bentham was also well ahead of his time when
it came to the issue of women’s rights.

00:17:40.540 --> 00:17:45.010
In fact, he once stated that it was the inequality
in the treatment of women that he observed

00:17:45.010 --> 00:17:49.490
in his youth that first prompted him to take
on the role of life-long reformer.

00:17:49.490 --> 00:17:53.470
Even though he was of the belief that women
were both physically and intellectually weaker

00:17:53.470 --> 00:18:00.650
than men, his utilitarian philosophy prescribed
that they should be able to enjoy equal rights.

00:18:00.650 --> 00:18:05.590
The Auto Icon

00:18:05.590 --> 00:18:09.490
As he approached his eighties, Bentham began
to give serious consideration to his death

00:18:09.490 --> 00:18:10.680
and legacy.

00:18:10.680 --> 00:18:14.920
He wrote detailed directions regarding what
was to be done with his corpse.

00:18:14.920 --> 00:18:19.180
In 1830, he wrote a paper in which he instructed
to the English physician Thomas Southward

00:18:19.180 --> 00:18:24.520
Smith to create what he called an auto-icon
and this was attached to his will.

00:18:24.520 --> 00:18:29.280
Jeremy Bentham died on June 6th, 1832 at his
home in Westminster, London.

00:18:29.280 --> 00:18:32.660
According to his instructions, his body was
dissected in the presence of his friends.

00:18:32.660 --> 00:18:37.370
The auto icon was created by reconstructing
the skeleton, replacing the head with a wax

00:18:37.370 --> 00:18:41.890
model, and then dressed up in his clothes
and placed on a seat in a glass case.

00:18:41.890 --> 00:18:46.760
This effigy, along with the mummified head
of Bentham is still on display at the University

00:18:46.760 --> 00:19:04.990
College in London.

